course: dictionary
lesson: 5178
title: Small word, big job: it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word it is a stand-in. It takes the place of a thing, an animal or an idea, so you do not have to repeat yourself. «I have a ball; it is red.» Here it points back to the ball.
explain: It also has jobs with no thing at all. «It is raining.» «It is 3 o'clock.» «It is far.» Here it is used for weather, time and distance, and it does not point to anything.
explain: To find what it means, ask: what is it pointing to? In «Super glue sticks skin together in seconds, so only an adult uses it», it points back to super glue. Sometimes it points forward instead, as in «It is fun to swim», where it stands for swimming, which comes later.
explain: Everyday and casual talk has special phrases. «That's it» means that's all, or that's right. «Cut it out» means stop doing that now. Use these with friends, not in formal writing.
fact: It is a pronoun: it stands in for a thing, an animal or an idea.
fact: It can point back to something already named, or forward to something coming later.
fact: It is used for weather, time and distance, as in «It is raining».
fact: Its (no apostrophe) shows that something belongs to it, as in «its back legs».
fact: It's is a short form of it is, as in «it's cloudy».
exercise: In «You can't get burnt when it's cloudy», what does it point to? | The clouds' shadows | The sunburn | *The weather | Your big sister
exercise: In «Super glue sticks skin together in seconds, so only an adult uses it», what does it point back to? | Skin | *Super glue | Seconds | Eyelids
exercise: In «It is fun to swim», what does it point forward to? | The weather | *Swimming, which comes after it | A swimming pool | The sun
test: In «The Australian frilled lizard hisses, and if that fails it runs off on its back legs», what does the second it stand for? | The frill | The back legs | *The frilled lizard | The predators
test: In casual talk, what does «That's it» mean? | Stop doing that now | This is the moment | You are the one in the game of tag | *That's all, or that's right
exam: In the formal opening «It follows that …», what does it point forward to? | *The idea that comes after, in the rest of the sentence | A shoe | The followers of a rule | The sun
